  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Peraton obligated $1.8B in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Peraton won 267 awards so far, with an average award size of $6.6M, and obligations were down 15.7% year over year. Peraton’s obligations by fiscal year show growth through FY25: FY21 was $466M, FY22 was $1.1B, FY23 was $1.7B, FY24 was $2B, FY25 was $2.1B, and FY26 is $1.8B to date. Peraton’s top buying agencies in FY2026 to date include the General Services Administration with $654M and the Department of Defense with $363M, followed by the Department of Transportation at $210M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $183M. Peraton’s largest NAICS markets in FY2026 to date are Computer Systems Design Services at $862M and Engineering Services at $385M, with Custom Computer Programming Services at $152M and Other Computer Related Services at $124M. The numbers reported here are contract obligations, except where award-value figures are noted as total contract values in other tables.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Peraton's federal business trended recently?",
        "body": "Peraton’s obligations rose from $466M in FY21 to $2.1B in FY25, then moved to $1.8B in FY26 to date. Peraton’s obligations were down 15.7% year over year for the current fiscal-year-to-date period. Peraton’s average award size in FY2026 to date is $6.6M across 267 awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Peraton's biggest federal customers and markets?",
        "body": "The General Services Administration is Peraton’s largest buyer in FY2026 to date at $654M, followed by the Department of Defense at $363M and the Department of Transportation at $210M. Computer Systems Design Services is Peraton’s largest NAICS market at $862M, followed by Engineering Services at $385M and Custom Computer Programming Services at $152M."
      }
    ]
